1
00:00:00,460 --> 00:00:04,420
So, last time, we saw how ANOVA

2
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:09,760
allowed us to compare means
across more than two groups.

3
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:15,660
So, we have a factor that defines
several groups, several levels.

4
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:20,380
And when our variables
are normally distributed,

5
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:26,900
we typically choose ANOVA to compare the means

6
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:31,360
across these different groups. Here,
the null hypothesis is that the mean

7
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:35,000
of group 1 equals the mean of
group 2 and the mean of group 3.

8
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:39,680
If we reject this null hypothesis,
with a p-value below 0.05,

9
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:45,560
we accept the alternative hypothesis
that one of these means is different,

10
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:47,900
statistically different from the other two.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,440
If, on the other hand, our data are
not normally distributed, we will

12
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:59,240
prefer the nonparametric equivalent of
ANOVA, which here would be Kruskal-Wallis.

13
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:07,610
Today, we will see how to generalize
this ANOVA with one factor and

14
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:12,830
several levels to an ANOVA with
several factors defining the groups.

15
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:20,410
Here, this graph shows means
plotted according to one factor

16
00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:27,810
and the group means for that
factor, and vertically, a

17
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:35,050
second factor and the means for
the levels of that second factor.

18
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:38,030
With an ANOVA like this, with several factors,

19
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:41,270
we are ultimately testing
several hypotheses at once.

20
00:01:41,410 --> 00:01:47,750
The effect of factor 1 on the
mean: that the mean of each

21
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:53,510
group of factor 1 is equal; and the
effect of factor 2 on the means,

22
00:01:53,510 --> 00:01:58,270
where all the groups of factor
2 would have the same mean.

23
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:04,690
We test these hypotheses twice, with
two associated p-values, and we can

24
00:02:04,690 --> 00:02:12,170
reject the effect of factor 1,
factor 2, or both in the result.
